the german parliament is expected to  approve the country's supply chain law  on friday it aims to make corporations  accountable for every inch  of their supply chains and any rights or  environmental abuses  that take place within them  uighur is picking cotton in china's  xinjiang region  the muslim population here has been  oppressed by beijing for many years  abused put in internment camps and made  to work in forced labor  much of their product used to end up in  shoes and shirts sold in germany and  other western countries  the supply chain law prohibits forced  labor as well as child labour and work  threatening or destroying the  environment  it applies to all companies selling to  germany  and it's met opposition from industry  bodies  is fearful we think it's wrong to  respond to a global problem  with a national law this doesn't change  the global human rights situation  all it does it creates additional  competitive disadvantages  for our own companies here which are  global leaders in terms of their  standards  the standards  the industry argues that german  companies might have to stop doing  business with some countries  making the situation for people they are  even worse  but berlin wants change  for too long we have accepted the  exploitation of people and nature in  developing countries  for too long we have tolerated child  labour on a large scale  children laboring on plantations or in  mines the german government wants to end  all that  and set an example for others in the eu  and beyond  that it can be done well let's get more  on this from our financial correspondent  in frankfurt chelsea delaney chelsea  businesses have argued  this law will be impossible to implement  are they right  well in its purest form it would be very  difficult because the idea  is that a company operating here in  germany would be responsible  from the moment that that raw material  from the moment that cotton or that uh  that metal  comes out of the ground in some far away  uh  but in actuality it's not going to be  that strict of a law for most companies  these  businesses are going to be responsible  for  themselves and their direct suppliers  but  further down the line they're indirect  suppliers they they only have to  uh basically conduct these due diligence  and risk analysis uh procedures if they  get some sort of  tip or a complaint that these human  rights are being violated so  this is still quite difficult for some  companies that haven't been doing this  yet  but this rule will only apply to 0.1  percent of german businesses  we've heard complaints from industry  bodies but it's not totally without  support from the business world is it  right and some of germany's biggest  companies have come out in support of  this  uh companies like daimler and adidas and  in bmw  have called for supply chain laws these  are companies that have to some extent  been  doing this sort of due diligence for  suppliers for a long time  but others are are still quite critical  of this they say especially for the  smaller businesses it's going to be very  difficult to build up  these new reporting systems and of  course many companies are quite worried  that this is going to put them at a  disadvantage when they have all of these  new bureaucratic costs and other  companies around the world don't  so are we expecting other countries to  follow in germany's footsteps  yeah i think many people think germany  will be a trendsetter for this france  already has a supply chain law like this  the eu  has basically promised to come out with  its own version  they had said june so i think we could  expect something like that soon  other countries have made some sort of  move towards supply chain diligence in  the u.s they've  banned the importing of cotton from  xinjiang  and other countries have made steps like  that as well but  i think we're a while away from a really  global  supply chain law like this chelsea  delaney and frankfurt thank you
 

